A||N||39||87ECO5||canine epileptic seizures||||4||
Z||000000||87ECO5||01-31-2001||12:26 AM||||Anyone here have a dog that has these?  Our golden retriever has had them occasionally (probably 4 - that we're aware of - in the past 4 years).  <P>However, she's had one every night for the past three!  Monday night's episode hit right at 23:00 and lasted about two minutes.  Then, last night, she had one at midnight.  And tonight, it hit right at 23:30.<P>She's going to see the vet tomorrow (today - I just looked at the clock!) but I thought I'd see if anyone here has had experience with this already.  I've read a little about this and it appears there's medication and surgery available that can prolong/prevent it.<P>Any information will be greatly appreciated.||||reg||4
Z||000001||frontal lobe||01-31-2001||01:18 AM||||When I was going up we had a saint bernard and later in life she started having seizures.  It was SO sad.<P>I have a friend who currently has a black labrador retriever with a seizure disorder.<P>Dogs can take anti-seizure medicines just like humans:  dilantin (phenytoin), phenobarbital, tegretol (carbamazepine).  In fact, most drugs are tested on animals before being tested on humans-first prison inmates and then the people of Louisiana.  OK, I was just joking about the human testing.  I'm not even sure why I picked Louisiana.  Just being silly.  The medicines can often make your dog dopey and/or clumsy.  Dosage may need to be adjusted several times.<P>I'm unaware of the complications of doing surgery on the poor dogs brains to eradicate the focal area of the brain triggering the seizures.  Must be expensive, though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||01-31-2001||05:47 AM||||My Collie had them... It was pretty sad.  We had to give him pills in a chunk of hotdog every morning.  I don't think he ever had another one, although we weren't watching him 24/7.  We put him to sleep last summer because he couldn't walk very well anymore and he'd yelp when he sat down sometimes.  He was 18 years old(people years.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||01-31-2001||06:09 AM||||Frontal, shame on you! did you forget to take your Zyprexa?? People are already paranoid about drug companies, and you throw more fuel on the fire!! LOL. (you comments were funny).<P>Pharmacists generally see lots of prescriptions for chronic dog ilnesses, like<BR>seizures and thyroid problems.  Phenobarb is very very cheap and is normally the drug of choice for seizures in dogs. The vet will want to do an ECG first to determine if that is the cause. Your dog will be shaved in a couple of small spots and then wires hooked up.  Kind of like hooking up a scanner to your fiero!! One of my dogs lately had something simular happen three days in a row. The ECG was negative.  The Vet had know idea, but blamed it on the cold weather.  The dog never did it again.<P>Let us know what he/she says (the vet).<P>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||01-31-2001||06:13 AM||||I saw something on tv about that the other day. I can't remember all details but I do remember that they have very good medication nowadays and that if you start with medication in the early stages of the disease, the dog can still lead a long a relatively normal live.<P>I would check the newsgroups for any additional information. Just go to <A HREF="http://www.deja.com/usenet" TARGET=_blank>http://www.deja.com/usenet</A>  and do a search on the symptoms.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||01-31-2001||08:49 AM||||That's really sad, Scott.<P>My dad had a dog with epilepsy in the 70's/early 80's...the vet prescribed Dilantin, which we gave the dog in marshmallows.  It took care of the seizures for about 10 years.<P>Hope everything comes out OK.||||reg||1
Z||000006||p4n1c||01-31-2001||08:49 AM||||My parents dog(part collie, part lab) has been getting them.  They seemed to have died down, but she keeps getting them.  Don't know how much longer she'll last.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||01-31-2001||09:17 AM||||Scott,<P>My parents Golden used to have them.  Our vet is in Middletown and I highly recommend them (Krebs, Kohler & Minnix).  Sam was on midication for so many things... I can't remember when and why they started.  He lived to 14 yrs.  For a Golden that was plagued with health problems his whole life... that is not bad.  <P>My mother would probably know more about this subject.  If you would like, I could call her.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000008||87ECO5||01-31-2001||09:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Our vet is in Middletown and I highly recommend them (Krebs, Kohler & Minnix).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We use the same vets - have for years.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TheFiero64||01-31-2001||02:04 PM||||My dad's 1 year old black lab had 2. I think he is on pills now. It is scary when it happens. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||87ECO5||01-31-2001||02:54 PM||||Just got back from the vet.  They did a physical (good results) and took some blood.  On a temporary basis (tonight and tomorrow morning only), they've got her on Phenobarbitol.  After the blood work comes back (and looks normal?), they plan to keep her on the Pheno for about two weeks.<P>The vet said, in most cases, the medication can be pulled after a few weeks.  Apparently, keeping the dog on the pills for only a few weeks can either prolong the episodes or eliminate them entirely.<P>Thanks to those that showed concern for her.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TheFiero64||01-31-2001||02:56 PM||||I will be praying for the best.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||frontal lobe||01-31-2001||11:44 PM||||This goes back a long way, so I just remembered, but the vet seemed to think our saint bernard's seizures might be triggered by giving her cheese.  I don't know why, and I don't remember that not giving her cheese stopped them.<P>Phenobarbital will work well, but just watch to see if your dog gets lethargic or sleepy or dopey, and a little clumsy.  Hopefully your dog will only need a few weeks treatment.  There are a fair number of cases where the dog needs it life long, but hopefully your won't.<P>RossT, yes I'm having trouble getting my Zyprexa in on a regular basis because my pharmacist won't let me take it at the same time as my lithium, ritalin, and haldol!||||reg||1
Z||000013||fropuff||01-31-2001||11:53 PM||||I hope everything goes alright.  It's weird, but everyone treats their pets like members of their families.  I know we did.  We had our 13-year-old dog, Skippy, put to sleep in August as a result of a stroke.  There was a bad thunderstorm, and he was afraid of thunder, and it scared him so much that he had one.  I was working on the Fiero and I heard him crying in the woods.  I went back there, and he couldn't stand up.  We rolled him onto a tarp and carried him out of the woods; he still couldn't walk or stand.  It was the saddest thing I've ever seen in my entire life; a dog that I knew and cared so much for in 13 years being put in my grandpa's van and driving away to the vet.  They couldn't do anything for him, so we had to put him to sleep.  The only thing that was good was that he did not suffer at all during the whole ordeal.  They said that he was strangely calm at the vet.  I hope I didn't bring you down, but I wanted to say that.  Sorry.  I'm hoping for the best.  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RossT||02-01-2001||12:30 AM||||Cheese has a substance called "tyramine" in it.  It is an amino acid.  Its also found in <BR>red wine and some other foods that I can't think of.  If humans are on an MAO inhibitor (monoamine-oxidase) for depression and you eat cheese, you may very well have a seizure and die.  Thats why alot of over the counter drugs warn you not to take their product if you are on an MAO inhibitor.  It could very well be that dogs can not metabolize tyramine, in which case if you gave your dog cheese, it could be the reason for a seizure.<BR>I am just speculating, and am unsure as to the mechanism for this.  My dogs love cheese!<P>Frontal, you need a new pharmacist, just find one that works nights in a hospital, and plays on the internet.  He/she could get you what you need.  Also ARE YOU GOING TO THE DELL RUN THIS YEAR?  May 31, June,1,2,3.||||reg||1
Z||000015||87ECO5||02-01-2001||07:34 AM||||No seizure last night!  WOOHOO!  I guess the Pheno worked!<P>Thanks to everyone for their help and support through this!  Maxi is not my dog, she's one of my kids.  And it kills me to see her going through what she did - even though the doctors claim she doesn't know it's happening.<P>I also want to take a moment to give my wife ( <B>FieroWife</B> ) some credit.  She had to deal with this by herself (when Max had them during the day while I was at work) and she handled it VERY well!  I, on the other hand, got to see it for the first time Sunday night and it scared the living $#^! out of me.||||reg||14
Z||000016||frontal lobe||02-01-2001||10:22 AM||||The vet is right that when the seizure is going on, your dog doesn't know what's happening and hence is not suffering.  Some dogs (and humans) get a sensation before the seizure and can tell it's coming, and that can be scary.  But that doesn't last long.  The ones that suffer the most are the ones that witness it, because it is frightening to watch.<P>RossT, yes, this time I PLAN to definitely try to make it to the Dell's run.  I hope to get to meet you there.||||reg||1
Z||000017||87ECO5||03-14-2001||12:44 PM||||UPDATE!<P>After several weeks of being on the Phenobarbital (and not having any problems), the worst one I've ever seen (and, apparently, she's ever experienced) hit last night.<P>It lasted about 15 minutes with the after-effects staying around close to an hour afterwards.  This happened around 12:30 this morning.  To make matters worse, when the second one hit at 4:00 (she's never had two in one night), she hadn't fully recovered from the 12:30 one (she was unable to fully use her left legs).<P>Had her at the vet this morning when they opened.  Doctor did a pretty thorough exam.  His diagnosis:  <B>A STROKE!!!</B><P>I was doing OK until he asked to see her walk.  I put her on the tile.  Her legs slid out from underneath.  She didn't move.  He shook his head, said "We've got a big problem here" and walked out of the room.  I burst into tears!<P>He offered to send her to the neurological center at OSU.  However, he said, and I quote:  "It will cost several hundred dollars to do and the probability of them finding something they can fix is quite low."<P>The appointment ended with him saying this to me:  "As long as she's eating, drinking and able to go to the bathroom, she could live like this for several more years.  In fact, as long as it doesn't get any worse, she will adapt quite well to not having full use of her left side.  However, one more episode like the one she received last night could cripple her to the point of having to put her down."<P>I walked out an hour later with three different medications (including instructions to double her intake of Phenobarbital), $28 fewer dollars in my wallet, and a very sick feeling in my stomach that my Golden's days could be numbered.<P>Thanks for "listening."||||reg||9
Z||000018||ChadMan||03-14-2001||12:57 PM||||Scott, that's horrible news and I'm really sorry.||||reg||9
Z||000019||Cheever2||03-14-2001||12:58 PM||||We're with ya, Scott.  Sorry for your family and for the dog.  Let us know if anything changes.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000020||Black88GT||03-14-2001||03:10 PM||||How much phenobarb do they have her on?  1/4 grain or 1/2 grain twice daily?  I have been a vet tech for 4 years now, I remember hearing something about too much phenobarb being not good.  I work tonight so if you want I could ask my boss and see what he says.||||reg||5
Z||000021||87ECO5||03-14-2001||03:15 PM||||Thanks, guys.<P>Black88GT - she was on 30mg twice a day.  It's now 60mg twice a day.  According to our vet, 60mg is the maximum amount a dog of her size (80 pounds) can handle.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 03-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||fropuff||03-14-2001||04:19 PM||||I am so terribly sorry.  I'm hoping that they can figure something out for her.  Very sorry to hear that.  ||||reg||1
Z||000023||TheFiero64||03-14-2001||05:50 PM||||Sorry to hear that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> We are all pulling for her.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<p>[This message has been edited by TheFiero64 (edited 03-14-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000024||Tigger||03-15-2001||08:23 AM||||Awe man, sorry to hear.  The golden I had as a kid had seizures too.<P>I've seen wheeled carts w/slings made for dogs with leg disabilites to help them get around.  Might be an option to consider...<P>I'm pulling for her too.||||reg||9
Z||000025||FieroWife||03-15-2001||12:44 PM||||Thank you all so much for all of the well wishes.  Today, Maxi is doing much better.  She's getting around better than she was yesterday and is eating and drinking just fine.  I got teary eyed reading all the wishes for our doggie!  Thank you!!!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/fierowife/Signature.gif"> ||||reg||7
Z||000026||fropuff||03-15-2001||05:04 PM||||That's great to hear.  Good luck!||||reg||4
Z||000027||frontal lobe||03-15-2001||11:41 PM||||For those of you not used to converting avoirdupois to metric, 1 grain is about equal to 60mg. (technically 1 grain=64.8mg), but often rounded off.||||reg||1
Z||000028||rodmcneill||03-16-2001||12:40 AM||||I hope your dog (family member) gets better soon.||||reg||1
Z||000029||87ECO5||06-24-2001||10:00 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/max1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/max2.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/max3.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/max4.jpg"> <P>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<BR>.<P><B>Maxi Leigh Haas<BR>May 5th, 1993 - June 24th, 2001</B><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000030||87 Pegasus||06-24-2001||10:06 PM||||Let me break it down.  Your dog will probably have to get blood tests done.  In over the three years that I have worked for a vet, I have never known a dog to have an ECG.  The most likely thing is blood test, followed by some perscription meds.  As someone stated, the doses may have to ge increased or decresed.  But once you figure out what is wrong and the right amount/drug that works for your dog, things should be alright.  You will have to take your dog in I believe at least once/twice a year for blood tests to make sure that the medication is not screwing anything else up on your pet.  Hope this helps you out.  Any questions, feel free to email me.  I'm using my boyfriends screen name: mkroyer007@aol.com.  Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Friends don't let friends drive Corvettes!!||||reg||13
Z||000031||87 Pegasus||06-24-2001||10:11 PM||||I just remembered something.  Your dog may not have just had a stroke.  There is such a thing as Old Dog Degenerative Disease....I am not EXACTLY sure of the title.  Basically it comes on JUST like a stroke, but the dog can recover in as little as two weeks.  This happened to one of the clients at my clinic.  Weirdest thing I have ever seen.  <P>------------------<BR>Friends don't let friends drive Corvettes!!||||reg||1
Z||000032||Black88GT||06-24-2001||10:17 PM||||87 Pegasus, maybe you should re-read his last post.  <P>Sorry for the loss Scott.||||reg||9
Z||000033||tgowens||06-24-2001||10:37 PM||||Scott, I am really sorry that Maxi is gone. She was a beautiful dog and a wonderful pet and I know that you and Lisa and the kids will miss her tremendously. I am glad that I had the chance to visit with her when I was there.||||reg||1
Z||000034||FieroJoe||06-24-2001||11:11 PM||||I'm very sorry.  My yellow lab, Sam, means a lot to me.  She is definatly a family member.  Losing her would devastate all of us.<P>I hope you pull through all right, I know exactly how much a dog can mean to someone.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000035||DaRkLoRD||06-25-2001||01:24 AM||||I'm afraid I'll understand exactly what you're going through very soon..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>My dog has cancer.. and last night he stopped eating. wouldn't touch his dinner, won't even eat dry crackers anymore. I know animals don't eat when they're sick, but even when he was sick before, he always at least had crackers..<P>I dunno what else to say.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||9
